WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
Start with an arbitrary coefficient
row, h zero through h n minus one.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.220
Encode it as the polynomial h of X,
with those entries as coefficients.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.200
We work with X to the n
equal to one. Multiply by X.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.740
Every exponent increases by one.
The last term reaches X to the n,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.640
which becomes one. So the last
coefficient moves to the first position,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.960
and every other coefficient
moves one position to the right.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.640
This is exactly one right cyclic shift.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:36.360
For our running example, h is negative
one plus X plus X squared plus X cubed.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:42.320
Multiplying by X gives one minus X plus
X squared plus X cubed. Its coefficients,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.160
one, negative one, one, one, form the
second row of the original matrix.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:53.040
We have not rearranged the example.
The same reasoning works for any power.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.160
Multiplication by X to the r
shifts the row r places right.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.340
The coefficient at X to the
j becomes h sub j minus r,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.840
with its index reduced
modulo n. The first row is h,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.600
the second is X h, and row j is
X to the j minus one times h.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.920
After the nth row, one more
multiplication returns to h.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.960
For a precise indexing formula, number
both rows and columns from zero.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.580
Then the entry in row r, column
j is h sub j minus r modulo n.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.460
In dimension four, row two,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.180
column one traces back to coefficient
three of the original row.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.640
This translates the entire circulant
structure into multiplication,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.540
and prepares us to compare dot
products of arbitrary rows.
